{"text": "Begin with the long sleeves. In the late Edo period, the writer Kitagawa Morisada sorted the robes of his day into four grades, from formal down to everyday. For a woman's formal dress, the older the wearer, the quieter the pattern. One study of a kimono magazine calls the long-sleeved robe the kimono a Japanese woman today is most likely ever to buy. Sleeves could also be cut down. In wartime, brides wore a black furisode with a train because its sleeves could later be shortened to make a tome-sode, a married woman's formal robe. A survey in one Tottori city in the late 1960s counted two hundred and six different crests. The jacket is the easiest member to borrow. In Kyoto's flea markets, visitors take to the haori because it needs no obi and no wrapping. Rules reached even the plainest cloth. Edo law told peasants to wear plain dark blue, and the order went out across the whole country. Each rule had its date. The next stretch of wall walks through them.", "duration": 70.64, "timestamps": {"word": [{"word": "Begin", "start": 0.12, "end": 0.4}, {"word": "with", "start": 0.44, "end": 0.56}, {"word": "the", "start": 0.6, "end": 0.68}, {"word": "long", "start": 0.78, "end": 0.98}, {"word": "sleeves.", "start": 1.12, "end": 1.62}, {"word": "In", "start": 2.18, "end": 2.26}, {"word": "the", "start": 2.28, "end": 2.34}, {"word": "late", "start": 2.4, "end": 2.64}, {"word": "Edo", "start": 2.76, "end": 3.06}, {"word": "period,", "start": 3.16, "end": 3.54}, {"word": "the", "start": 3.8, "end": 3.88}, {"word": "writer", "start": 3.9, "end": 4.26}, {"word": "Kitagawa", "start": 4.42, "end": 5.2}, {"word": "Morisada", "start": 5.26, "end": 5.94}, {"word": "sorted", "start": 6.06, "end": 6.4}, {"word": "the", "start": 6.44, "end": 6.52}, {"word": "robes", "start": 6.58, "end": 6.82}, {"word": "of", "start": 6.86, "end": 6.94}, {"word": "his", "start": 6.98, "end": 7.14}, {"word": "day", "start": 7.2, "end": 7.46}, {"word": "into", "start": 7.52, "end": 7.74}, {"word